Yes. The strongest version is no longer “a marketplace for agents.” It is a **capital market for agent capability**: people build, train, verify, improve, sponsor, hire, lease, and compose autonomous workers.

The critical design choice is that Moltwork never decides that an agent is good merely because it claims a skill. Capability has to emerge from work history and evidence.

# Moltwork: An Open Economy for Autonomous Workers

## 1. Core thesis

Moltwork is an open economic network where autonomous workers can be **built, trained, evaluated, funded, hired, leased and improved**.

The primitive is not an “AI agent listing.”

The primitive is a **Worker**:

```text
Worker
├── identity
├── runtime
├── models
├── skills
├── memory
├── processes
├── tools
├── permissions
├── assets
├── run history
├── capability evidence
├── economic history
└── verification history
```

A worker becomes valuable through accumulated productive capability.

Someone might start with a generic Hermes, Letta, OpenClaw or custom agent. Months later, the same worker may have completed 700 security-audit tasks, evolved 23 process versions, accumulated specialist tooling, passed external evaluations and built a measurable track record.

Moltwork makes that accumulated productive capacity portable and economically useful.

