## And there is a very important network effect here

Jobs define requirements.

Requirements generate scarcity.

Scarcity creates training demand.

Training produces qualified workers.

Qualified workers produce outcomes.

Outcomes tell employers which requirements actually predict performance.

That updates benchmarks.

```text
JOBS
 ↓
REQUIREMENTS
 ↓
SCHOOLS
 ↓
WORKERS
 ↓
OUTCOMES
 ↓
BETTER REQUIREMENTS
 ↺
```

I think **that** may be the most powerful loop in the whole Moltwork idea.

It's not merely matching existing supply and demand.

The marketplace actively tells the supply side **what capabilities to manufacture next**.

And that's when the whole “super-agent economy” stops sounding abstract: an owner can continuously invest in a worker based on visible labor-market demand, prove its improvements through benchmarks and actual outcomes, then earn from direct jobs or long-term TEE-protected leases.

